============================================================
2026-06-29: Cross-sectional feature normalization experiment
============================================================
Question raised: features like RSI aren't comparable across the pooled
universe (RSI 60 is stretched for a quiet utility, normal for a growth
name), and raw MACD is in dollar units so high-priced stocks dominate.
Should we normalize features for the single pooled model?

Key fact about XGBoost: trees are invariant to monotonic transforms of a
single feature, so a global StandardScaler / min-max does NOTHING. Only
PER-STOCK or CROSS-SECTIONAL normalization (which changes the relative
ordering of rows) can move the model.

Tried three changes, kept originals alongside, A/B'd via the existing
walk-forward + holdout (technical-only, no news):
  1. MACD / price                   (macd_norm, fixes a real $-units bug)
  2. Per-stock RSI z-score          (rsi_z, 63-day window)
  3. Vol-normalized returns/slopes  (/ 20-day realized daily vol)

Result (AUC):
                      walk-forward    holdout (unseen tickers)
  baseline (orig)        0.496           0.510
  all 3 normalized       0.501           0.484   <- holdout WORSE
  macd_norm only         0.499           0.508   <- neutral

Conclusion: normalization did not help. The all-3 variant slightly HURT
the honest out-of-ticker metric (-0.026). Everything sits at ~0.50 AUC,
so the binding constraint is upstream of feature scaling — there's barely
a signal to normalize. Confirms the ~0.49 ceiling is structural, not a
feature-engineering problem.

Decision: kept ONLY macd_norm (replacing the raw dollar MACD columns,
since feeding dollar-denominated values into a pooled model is just wrong
even though it's metric-neutral). Reverted rsi_z and the vol-normalized
returns/slopes. Don't re-run this experiment expecting a different result
unless the underlying signal/label changes first.

TODO: stop tuning features against ~0.50 AUC. The next lever is the label
/ horizon (5-day direction may be too noisy) or cross-sectional ranking
evaluation ("did the top basket beat the bottom?"), not more feature
scaling.


============================================================
2026-06-29: Do walk_forward_validate and walk_forward_holdout both
            actually use the model? (asked: "see if both use the model")
============================================================
YES — both fit and score an XGBoost model. Verified in src/models/train.py:

  - walk_forward_validate (~line 97) delegates folds to _walk_forward_folds
    (~line 75), which per fold does:
        model = _build_model()                              # train.py:91
        model.fit(train[feature_cols], train[LABEL_COL])    # :92
        proba = model.predict_proba(test[...])[:, 1]        # :93
    So validate trains a fresh model each fold and scores the held-out
    DATES of the SAME (train) universe. walk_forward_backtest reuses the
    exact same _walk_forward_folds, so it uses the model too.

  - walk_forward_holdout (~line 260) has its own loop but does the same:
        model = _build_model()                              # train.py:282
        model.fit(tr[feature_cols], tr[LABEL_COL])          # :283
        proba = model.predict_proba(te[...])[:, 1]          # :284
    Difference: it fits on TRAIN-universe rows up to the cutoff and scores
    the HOLDOUT tickers in the next block — out-of-sample on BOTH dates
    and tickers.

Important nuance: neither loads the saved production model
(models/saved/pooled_xgb.joblib). They each build EPHEMERAL models per
fold via _build_model() (same XGBClassifier config). Only
train_pooled_model() persists the joblib, and only predict.py loads it.
So "use the model" = same model configuration/algorithm, fit per fold —
not the on-disk artifact. Both evaluators are therefore measuring the
model's learning procedure, which is what you want for validation.
